87
00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:56.800
Richard Bair: is the one that we… we had, maybe a little… slightly premature bringing it, but not really. This was really straightforward, so let me just explain it. So, the problem that we have, oftentimes, is that, the, as the size of the council has grown.

88
00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:11.000
Richard Bair: Each transaction is a multi-sync transaction, and we get a lot of transaction signatures. And those transaction signatures take a certain number of bytes, and when you start multiplying it all out, we often get transactions that are larger than 6 kilobytes in size.

89
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:28.610
Richard Bair: And so those transactions actually cannot be submitted to the network. So what we have to do instead is we analyze all the transactions that we've gathered, or all the signatures that we've gathered, and we try to filter out the signatures so that we just have the minimum set that we need in order to succeed, and then we create a transaction that way and submit it to the network.

90
00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:37.219
Richard Bair: But even this is unlikely to work as the Council fills its full 39 seats. There's just not a lot of room for data in those transactions.

91
00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:42.810
Richard Bair: And so, since we introduced jumbo transactions, jumbo Ethereum transactions.

92
00:12:42.810 --> 00:13:05.610
Richard Bair: The thought was, is that eventually we would, you know, investigate all the different types of transactions that we do, and decide which ones could also be larger than the 6KB limit. And this particular hip is to just make it so that if one of the privileged accounts on the network, and this is, you know, for Hedera Council, this is an important feature, but also in any sort of.

93
00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:16.400
Richard Bair: other standalone network, HIRO network, that gets set up, whoever's managing that network would have the ability to submit larger transactions. So there's

94
00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:31.450
Richard Bair: These two privileged accounts, 002 and 0050, and both of them would be able to submit, larger than 6 kilobytes. And so, the proposal is to use the same jumbo transaction size.

95
00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:37.399
Richard Bair: As the maximum, which basically just means that we cap it at the same size as demo transactions.

96
00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:38.640
Richard Bair: Oh…

97
00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:50.270
Richard Bair: And, and that'd be it. There wouldn't be any change to any other transactions, just the ones that are signed by, the network administrators or privileged accounts on the network.

