131
00:17:53.930 --> 00:17:54.550
Alex Popowycz: Okay.

132
00:17:54.550 --> 00:18:03.519
Richard Bair: we could have… we could have, you know, there are limits on accounts for things like, you know, how many keys you can attach to the account.

133
00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:23.840
Richard Bair: you know, so there are limits like that, but we don't do, like, a size limit. And the reason that there's a limit to the number of keys you can attach to an account isn't for size purposes, it's just because when we do, you know, verifications and things like that, there's a lot of data that we have to read up, and a lot of keys that we have to go through looking for the one that we're looking for.

134
00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:26.600
Alex Popowycz: Yeah, I mean, deserialization and all that kind of stuff.

135
00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:38.770
Richard Bair: And especially if you're doing, like, threshold keys, and you have, like, threshold keys of threshold keys of threshold keys, it can be sort of ridiculous. So we do have caps on that, but those caps are really big. They're, like, 10 levels deep, or something like that.

136
00:18:38.770 --> 00:18:40.609
Alex Popowycz: I think it's 15, yep.

137
00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:42.320
Alex Popowycz: Yeah. Okay.

138
00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:44.490
Richard Bair: Okay, cool.

139
00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:47.649
Richard Bair: Let me open up the next guy, then.

140
00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:58.190
Richard Bair: Okay, alright, so this is another one that's, near and dear to Alex's heart. So…

141
00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:15.229
Richard Bair: The, in… in the, in the system, we have support for what we call dynamic address books. The address book is the list of nodes and information about those nodes. And,

142
00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:34.319
Richard Bair: there's kind of three phases to the address book. The first phase was completely static. We had a file that was on every single node. Those files were all identical, and they listed all the different nodes that were in the address book, their public keys, their weights, all that kind of information.

143
00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:46.520
Richard Bair: Then we changed that to use an on, an on-chain data structure and a header API, or HIRO API, that you could use in order to,

144
00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:51.680
Richard Bair: Change which nodes are in and out, and actually have it all saved in state.

145
00:19:51.730 --> 00:20:04.519
Richard Bair: And, currently, we make changes to that address book, and then once a month, when we do our upgrades, basically upgrade, the new address book is adopted and used by all the different nodes.

146
00:20:04.520 --> 00:20:21.780
Richard Bair: And then the next phase that we will go into, what we call Dynamic Address Book Phase 3, is where we will do it on a nightly basis. Whenever we are doing, staking reward calculations, all that stuff, which we do first transaction after midnight UTC time.

147
00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:33.419
Richard Bair: We would also then adopt the latest address book. So this is why we call it the dynamic address book, and it'll allow nodes to arrive or leave or change their stake weights and all that kind of stuff on a daily basis.

148
00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:48.130
Richard Bair: So, anyway, inside the dynamic address book, there's a data structure that lists all these details about the node, and one of those details is the account ID associated with the node.

149
00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:57.700
Richard Bair: Now, the… the node of account ID is a really important part of the story. Basically, if you were a node operator.

150
00:20:57.750 --> 00:21:15.339
Richard Bair: you could have your own account listed with the node, or you can have, somebody else's node… sorry, somebody else's account associated with the node. For example, if I were a node operator, and I didn't want to get paid in crypto, but I wanted to be paid in euros.

151
00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:30.410
Richard Bair: I could contract with somebody else, I could put their account onto my node in the address book, and then all of the node rewards that I received would go to that account, and then they could cut me a check at the end of the month, for example.

152
00:21:30.470 --> 00:21:49.739
Richard Bair: Or maybe I'm the legal operator of the node, but there's somebody else who's actually physically operating the node, and they're supposed to get the rewards. Whatever. There's whatever arrangement you have. Or maybe it's your node, and you have 10 accounts, and you want to change which account is getting the node rewards. However you do it.

153
00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:55.249
Richard Bair: you want to be able to specify what account is getting the rewards, but that account doesn't just get the rewards.

154
00:21:55.250 --> 00:22:11.900
Richard Bair: That account also pays for fines. So if you are a node operator, and you're operating a node, and your node is a bad node, maybe you've modified the software in some way, and, you know, the network says, oh, you are doing something naughty, we're going to fine you.

155
00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:27.979
Richard Bair: Or maybe it's just under normal, there are fines that a node operator can receive, even if they are honest and operating normally, which is a little bit surprising the first time you hear from it. It's kind of a long story, but I'll give you the short version.

156
00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:41.949
Richard Bair: every time a user submits a transaction to the node, the node performs due diligence on that transaction, makes sure that it's a good transaction before it submits it to the network so that it goes to Gossip, and everybody does the Hashgraph algorithm.

157
00:22:41.950 --> 00:23:04.690
Richard Bair: comes to consensus, reaches consensus on it, and then executes the transaction. All that work that the network has to do, we can't allow that work to happen for free. Somebody has to pay for that work, because otherwise, you could have a denial of service. So, if a user submits their transaction to the node, and then the node does the due diligence check and submits it to the network under normal circumstances.

158
00:23:04.690 --> 00:23:20.429
Richard Bair: It means that the user who submitted the transaction will pay for that transaction, or the user who signed up the transaction as the payer will pay for that transaction, whether it succeeds, whether it fails, whether it's a duplicate, whether it's not a duplicate, whatever the scenario is, the payer will pay.

159
00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:28.740
Richard Bair: But, it is possible that, there's two scenarios that are possible. One is that the node didn't perform due diligence checks.

160
00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:42.289
Richard Bair: Some users submitted the transaction, and instead of checking to make sure that the payer signature is actually valid on that transaction, the node just said, hey, that's a little extra CPU, I don't want to do that, I'm just going to submit it to the network anyway.

161
00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:55.099
Richard Bair: And if they were to do that, then the node will be fined. Basically, we'll come to consensus, we'll get the transaction, we'll check the signatures, and we'll say, hey, this thing isn't signed properly. That node, maybe that node was…

162
00:23:55.140 --> 00:24:03.599
Richard Bair: lying to us, and sent us a transaction that wasn't proper. And so then the node will end up getting fined for that.

163
00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:11.440
Richard Bair: The other, the other thing that could happen is… so that's a case where the node actually did do something wrong, it gets fined.

164
00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:24.560
Richard Bair: But there's also a small little corner case where the node didn't actually do anything wrong, but they'll still get fined, because somebody has to pay for it, and the network can't tell the difference between this scenario and a malicious node scenario.

165
00:24:24.590 --> 00:24:34.259
Richard Bair: What can happen is that the user can submit their transaction, we do the payer signature check on the node, and at that moment in time, the signature is valid.

166
00:24:34.330 --> 00:24:48.780
Richard Bair: But maybe there was another transaction that got gossiped into the network. Maybe it didn't even come to this node, it came to some other node, and it started getting gossiped around. Maybe this node's never even seen that transaction yet, because it's all kind of asynchronous. And in that transaction, the keys on the account has changed.

167
00:24:48.780 --> 00:25:12.219
Richard Bair: So, at the moment that the node is looking at this user's transaction, the transaction is signed properly. When it submits it to the network, and it comes to consensus, turns out it reaches consensus after the transaction that changed the signature, you know, the keys on that account. And now, when it reaches consensus, that event is… or that transaction is no longer properly signed.

168
00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:25.909
Richard Bair: The network can't tell that this happens because the user did something wrong versus what the node that submitted the transaction did something wrong. So somebody has to pay. And what we decided was, in this case, the node will pay.

169
00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:33.170
Richard Bair: And, so it, it's, extremely unlikely to happen,

170
00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:42.099
Richard Bair: It's extremely unlikely to happen often, but it is just a little corner case, just to be aware of. So the important part is that this account…

171
00:25:42.230 --> 00:25:50.570
Richard Bair: associated with the node is responsible both for paying fines and for… or I should say, paying fees.

172
00:25:50.670 --> 00:26:04.870
Richard Bair: on behalf of the node, and for receiving node rewards. And so… and these could be two different entities. It could be one person who operates the node, and another person who's, operating the account. It could be two different entities.

173
00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:21.639
