00:40:04.350 --> 00:40:17.980
Richard Bair: So, Jason asked a question in the chat. With regards to the upcoming HIP, is a node ID really necessary in the transaction in the first place? Why should a client be required to fuse a transaction to a gossip node with its signature?

265
00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:27.999
Richard Bair: You already do not require a node ID for internal batch transactions, that would suggest that this requirement is not necessary. Yeah, so there's a reason for it. The reason is that,

266
00:40:28.150 --> 00:40:41.699
Richard Bair: When you submit your… let's say that I have, you know, 100 nodes in the network, and let's say that a lot of them are anonymous, and you submit a transaction, you sign the transaction, and you submit it.

267
00:40:41.900 --> 00:40:54.070
Richard Bair: Now, and let's say that there was no node ID in that transaction at all, or no node account ID. There wasn't any way to identify which node you were, trusting, basically, that you were sending this transaction to.

268
00:40:54.250 --> 00:41:06.429
Richard Bair: What could happen is that as soon as that transaction hit the honest node that you submitted it to, that honest node looks at it, says, yep, everything is good, puts it into the network for gossip.

269
00:41:06.860 --> 00:41:16.310
Richard Bair: When it goes into the network for gossip, it sort of bounces around, and a bunch of anonymous nodes, maybe dishonest nodes, see that transaction.

270
00:41:16.350 --> 00:41:19.830
Richard Bair: And they go, haha, look at this, wonderful transaction.

271
00:41:19.840 --> 00:41:37.360
Richard Bair: I will take that transaction and bundle it in one of my own events and send it as if the user had sent it to me. The user didn't send it to me, but I'll pretend that the user sent it to me, and I will gossip it around. And the reason I will do that is because I want to get a reward for receiving

272
00:41:37.360 --> 00:41:49.309
Richard Bair: the transaction from the user. Because every time… if you're a node, part of the node economics is that if you're a node and you receive a transaction, then you get a small portion of the fee.

273
00:41:49.310 --> 00:42:07.380
Richard Bair: goes to you for doing the work of ingesting that transaction, for maintaining your proxies and keeping your, you know, communication open and all that kind of stuff to accept transactions in the network. You get a small reward for that when somebody sends you a transaction. So if we didn't have a way to kind of fuse the

274
00:42:07.530 --> 00:42:22.450
Richard Bair: transaction to the node that the user intended to pay for that transaction, then any node on the network would be able to just grab that transaction and resend it as if the user had sent it to them, and they'd be able to claim a little reward for that.

275
00:42:22.570 --> 00:42:31.439
Richard Bair: And of course, the sauce nodes would do this a lot, and they'd be able to make money from it. It's also…

276
00:42:32.660 --> 00:42:39.029
Richard Bair: Well, that's the main… yeah, that's the main thing. It would have to happen at, sort of, at that point where…

277
00:42:39.030 --> 00:42:56.070
Richard Bair: you're still gossiping, because it… also, of course, your transaction will end up in an event stream, or a record stream, or a block stream, and anybody in the world would be able to take that transaction and resubmit it, and it would basically sort of be like vandalism. You just get to make that one user pay. But that shouldn't…

278
00:42:56.070 --> 00:43:11.019
Richard Bair: generally be possible, because we do deduplication checks as well, and the time checks. So, for the most part, I think the potential attack vector, if you didn't cite it, is that dishonest nodes would be able to force the user to pay for duplicate transactions.

279
00:43:11.210 --> 00:43:21.210
Richard Bair: That was kind of the game. We could remove the incentive for that by saying, well, nodes won't get a

280
00:43:21.220 --> 00:43:34.649
Richard Bair: reward for accepting transactions, but then you have sort of the tragedy of the commons, where it's not in the interest of any of the known operators individually to accept transactions from the outside world, you know.

281
00:43:35.150 --> 00:43:53.810
Richard Bair: If they didn't accept transactions from the outside world, they don't get DDoSed or any of that, they would still get rewarded. So, who wants to turn on proxies? Well, somebody has to. Well, who are those people? They're the ones that are going to take the hit, and if there's not enough of them, then our DDoS prevention isn't very good, and so forth. So you need some incentive to turn it on.

282
00:43:53.810 --> 00:43:56.180
Richard Bair: But even beyond that,

283
00:43:56.280 --> 00:44:11.269
Richard Bair: a bad guy could still just vandalize a user, basically, by just resubmitting their transactions over again as duplicates. So that was kind of the sort of fundamental reason as I understand it.

